NetBSD-on-AP040 live debugging notes (2026-08-21)
=================================================

The MiSTer's ARM can read the core's DDR3 window while the Amiga runs, so
a stuck NetBSD can be debugged from the outside with no cooperation from
the guest.  Everything here was used live against a hang at init-exec.

Memory map (ARM physical -> Amiga):
  0x2800_0000   Z3_0 fast RAM (128MB window)     [cpu_wrapper.v ramaddr]
  0x3000_0000   Z3_1 fast RAM (256MB window)
  chip/slow/motherboard fast are in SDRAM: NOT ARM-visible.
  DDR data is 16-bit byte-swapped relative to ARM byte order: swap every
  byte pair before interpreting.

Tools:
  memdump.py         mmap-based /dev/mem reader (dd read() fails past the
                     kernel map; mmap works).  scp to /tmp on the MiSTer.
  ksyms-9.2-GENERIC.txt  symbol addresses extracted from the OFFICIAL
                     NetBSD-9.2 amiga GENERIC kernel (mkrepro build, so it
                     bit-matches what users run).  Kernel VA == offset
                     into the Z3 segment: VA X is ARM (segment base + X).

Findings from the captured hang (kernel loaded in Z3_1):
  * the msgbuf (struct kern_msgbuf, magic 0x063061, top of the segment)
    showed boot got PAST the on-screen "kern.module.path=..." -- root
    mounted on wd0a (ffs), "WARNING: clock gained 20 days" printed --
    i.e. the hang is at EXEC OF /sbin/init: first user-mode process,
    first demand paging, first RTE to user space.
  * the machine was ALIVE: clock interrupts ticking, callout wheel
    advancing, ~1KB/8s of kernel data changing, no fault storm, no
    panic string.  A quiescent sleep, not a wedge and not a loop.
  * AmigaOS (no MMU, no user mode) boots on the same image, and its IDE
    interrupts work, which localizes suspicion to the user-mode/MMU
    transition rather than the disk path.
  * confound not yet excluded: that image closed clk_114 at +0.034 ns
    and X2.3 builds are consistently razor-thin there; a timing-clean
    build must reproduce the hang before it is chased as logic.

Capture BEFORE rebooting: the DDR window is repurposed the moment
another OS boots.
